## The rule

`super(...)` runs the base class constructor. In any subclass that declares its own constructor, the call to `super(...)` must be the first statement — before any use of `this`.

```ts
class Animal {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name: string, public breed: string) {
    super(name); // must be the first statement
    // breed is assigned by the parameter property, after super()
  }
}
```

## Why `this` is off-limits before `super()`

The base constructor builds the part of the object your subclass extends. Touching `this` before it runs means reading a half-constructed object, so the compiler rejects it:

```ts
class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name: string) {
    this.name = name; // Error: 'super' must be called before accessing 'this'
    super(name);
  }
}
```

## The implicit constructor

If a subclass declares no constructor at all, TypeScript generates one that forwards every argument to `super`:

```ts
class Puppy extends Animal {}
// behaves as: constructor(...args) { super(...args); }
new Puppy('Rex'); // fine
```

So you only write a constructor when the subclass adds its own parameters or setup.

## Calling base methods, not just the constructor

`super.method()` calls the parent's implementation — the usual reason is extending behaviour while overriding it:

```ts
class Logger {
  log(message: string) {
    console.log(message);
  }
}

class TimestampLogger extends Logger {
  override log(message: string) {
    super.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${message}`);
  }
}
```

The `override` keyword, with `noImplicitOverride` enabled, makes the intent explicit and catches the case where the base method is renamed out from under you.

## Template literals

A template literal is delimited by backticks and embeds expressions with `${...}`. It also spans multiple lines without `\n`:

```ts
const name = 'Aaron';
const count = 3;

const greeting = `Hi ${name}, you have ${count} new messages`;
const sum = `Total: ${count * 2}`;
const block = `Line one
Line two`;
```

Anything that is a valid expression can go inside `${}` — property access, calls, arithmetic, ternaries.

## What TypeScript checks

The result is always typed `string`. Each interpolated expression has to produce something that can convert to a string; an object with no meaningful `toString` becomes `'[object Object]'`, which is usually a bug rather than the intent. Prefer template literals over `+` concatenation — fewer quotes and no stray spacing mistakes.

## Tagged templates

A function placed before the literal receives the static parts and the interpolated values, so it can build a custom result. Libraries use this for SQL, styled-components, and i18n:

```ts
function upper(strings: TemplateStringsArray, ...values: unknown[]) {
  return strings.reduce(
    (acc, part, i) => acc + part + String(values[i] ?? '').toUpperCase(),
    '',
  );
}
```

## Template literal types

This is the TypeScript-specific part. The same `${}` syntax works in type positions, so a type can describe the exact strings a union produces:

```ts
type Lang = 'en' | 'tr';
type TranslationKey = `greeting.${Lang}`;
// 'greeting.en' | 'greeting.tr'

type Pixels = `${number}px`;
const gap: Pixels = '8px'; // ok
```

That is how libraries type route strings, CSS units, and event names without widening everything back to plain `string`.

## Module vs script

A file with no top-level `import` or `export` is a *script* — its top-level names leak into the global scope. Add a single `export` and the file becomes a *module* with its own scope. Modules are the default in any real project.

## Exporting

```ts
// named exports
export function formatDate(d: Date) {
  return d.toISOString();
}
export const MAX_RETRIES = 3;

// default export — at most one per file
export default class ApiClient {}

// re-export from another module
export { parseUser } from './user';
```

## Importing

```ts
import ApiClient, { formatDate, MAX_RETRIES } from './api';
import * as utils from './utils';
```

## Type-only imports and exports

Marking something as type-only tells the compiler it can be erased completely — it never reaches the bundle:

```ts
export type { User } from './types';
import type { User } from './types';
```

## Exposing a class outside its file

There is no separate visibility mechanism for this — you `export` it. Anything you do not export is module-private, and that is a feature: the export list is your encapsulation boundary.

```ts
function internalHelper() {} // private to this file

export class Service {       // visible to importers
  run() {
    internalHelper();
  }
}
```

## Resolution, briefly

How `'./api'` and bare specifiers like `'react'` resolve is controlled by tsconfig's `module` and `moduleResolution`, plus the bundler. Path aliases such as `@/lib/...` are just a configured shortcut for a folder.

## The problem generics solve

Consider a function that returns the first element of an array. Typed with `any`, it compiles but every caller loses type information:

```ts
function firstAny(arr: any[]): any {
  return arr[0];
}
const n = firstAny([1, 2, 3]); // n is 'any' — no checking from here on
```

Writing one version per type is duplication. A generic gives you a single definition that keeps the type:

```ts
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}
const n = first([1, 2, 3]);    // number | undefined
const s = first(['a', 'b']);   // string | undefined
```

`T` is a type parameter; the compiler infers it from the argument, so callers rarely write it explicitly.

## Constraints

`extends` restricts what `T` can be while keeping the function generic:

```ts
function getId<T extends { id: string }>(item: T): string {
  return item.id; // allowed — every T is guaranteed to have an id
}
```

## Generic interfaces and classes

```ts
interface Box<T> {
  value: T;
}

class Store<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  add(item: T) {
    this.items.push(item);
  }
}
```

## Default type parameters

```ts
interface ApiResult<T = unknown> {
  data: T;
  error: string | null;
}
```

## Where it pays off

Reusable utilities, data structures, API clients, and React component props — anywhere a definition should work over many types without falling back to `any`. The skill is using only as much generality as the API needs; over-generic code is as hard to read as under-typed code.

## Primitives

`string`, `number` (one numeric type — integers, floats, `NaN`, `Infinity`), `boolean`, `bigint`, `symbol`, and the two empty types `null` and `undefined`. Literal types are a refinement of these — `'GET' | 'POST'` is a `string` narrowed to two specific values.

```ts
const name: string = 'Aaron';
const count: number = 42;
let method: 'GET' | 'POST' = 'GET';
```

## Structural types

`object`, arrays, tuples, and function types:

```ts
const ids: number[] = [1, 2, 3];
const pair: [string, number] = ['age', 30]; // tuple — fixed length and order
type Handler = (event: string) => void;     // function type
```

## Special types

- `any` — opts out of type checking entirely. Anything is assignable to it and from it. Avoid it; it spreads silently through the code that touches it.
- `unknown` — the safe top type. Anything is assignable *to* it, but you must narrow it before you use it. The right choice for values of unknown shape, such as parsed JSON.
- `never` — the bottom type; no value is assignable to it. Used for functions that always throw and for exhaustiveness checks in a `switch`.
- `void` — the return type of a function that produces nothing meaningful.

```ts
let data: unknown = JSON.parse(input);
if (typeof data === 'string') {
  data.toUpperCase(); // ok — narrowed to string
}

function fail(message: string): never {
  throw new Error(message);
}
```

## any vs unknown vs never

`any` turns checking off, `unknown` forces you to check, `never` says a value cannot exist. Reach for `unknown` wherever you are tempted by `any` — it keeps the safety and costs only one narrowing step.
